From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jay Leaton <58495321+jayleaton@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 21 Nov 2026 14:59:15 +0700
Subject: [PATCH 44/46] flashnext cuda: TF_FLASHNEXT_PEER_SLOTS, the --tp 2 MoE
 exchange as owner-slot writes into the peer's buffer

At --tp 2 each MoE slot is non-zero on one rank only: a routed pair on the rank holding its expert, and each half
of the shared expert's columns on its rank under the shared split. The all-reduce (and, past SPLIT_ROWS, the
reduce-scatter) moves every slot anyway. With TF_FLASHNEXT_PEER_SLOTS=1 on one host, each rank instead writes only
the columns it owns into the peer's receive buffer, mapped once at load through CUDA IPC: about half the bytes.

- fn_peer_send: a block a pair, the rank's columns of y (each value plus 0, as the all-reduce leaves -0) into the
  peer's buffer; the last block raises the peer's epoch flag after a system fence.
- fn_peer_merge: waits for the peer's epoch (traps after about 15 s rather than hang), then the peer's columns from
  the buffer and this rank's from y, each plus 0. Two parity halves keep a write from meeting a merge still reading.
- A short pass sends and merges every row, then serves y as after the all-reduce; a split pass sends the peer's rows,
  merges its own and keeps the sums gather (cuda_moe_exchange.finish), so every sum keeps TP1's order.
- Passes past 128 rows keep the collectives. Refused across hosts and with TF_FLASHNEXT_DECODE_GRAPH (epochs are
  launch arguments); the rank identity carries the setting, so both ranks agree.
